The Ektara is a one-stringed drone instrument closely associated with the wandering Baul minstrels of Bengal. It has a resonating gourd or coconut-shell soundbox covered with skin, from which a bamboo neck rises, with a single string plucked to produce a rhythmic drone accompanying Baul devotional songs.

The other options don't fit:
the Kamaicha is a bowed, multi-stringed instrument of the Manganiyar community of Rajasthan;
the Pung is a barrel-shaped drum used in Manipuri dance forms like Pung Cholom;
"Niyara Hongsing Ke" isn't a recognized standard Indian classical/folk instrument name.
